; $VER: test_save.script 1.0 (05.11.2026)
; Does what TinyDev does when it saves a file, many times over, without
; TinyDev: if the file system is damaged afterwards, TinyDev is not the
; cause.
;
; Usage: Execute scripts/test_save.script DIR=<drawer> [TIMES=<n>] [SOURCE=<file>]
;   DIR     a drawer on the drive to test (made if it does not exist);
;           only the files test.txt, test.txt.tmp and test.txt.bak in it
;           are written
;   TIMES   how many saves (default 200)
;   SOURCE  the text written (default S:Startup-Sequence); every second
;           save writes it twice, so the files change their size
;
; One save, as file_save() in src/fileio.c does it:
;   write test.txt.tmp, give it the script bit and a comment,
;   rename test.txt to test.txt.bak, test.txt.tmp to test.txt,
;   delete test.txt.bak.
; Afterwards wait a few seconds (the file system writes its bitmap later),
; then reboot: a "block used twice" or "not validated" means the damage
; comes from the file system or the emulator. Rebooting at once instead
; shows whether a reset shortly after saving leaves it damaged.

.key DIR/A,TIMES/N,SOURCE
.bra @
.ket %
.def TIMES 200
.def SOURCE "S:Startup-Sequence"

FailAt 21
IF NOT EXISTS "@DIR%"
    MakeDir "@DIR%"
ENDIF
IF NOT EXISTS "@DIR%"
    Echo "Cannot make the drawer @DIR%."
    Quit 10
ENDIF

Set n 0
Lab next
Set n `Eval $n + 1`
IF $n GT @TIMES% VAL
    Skip done
ENDIF
Set odd `Eval $n MOD 2`
IF $odd EQ 1 VAL
    Copy "@SOURCE%" TO "@DIR%/test.txt.tmp" QUIET
ELSE
    Join "@SOURCE%" "@SOURCE%" AS "@DIR%/test.txt.tmp"
ENDIF
IF WARN
    Echo "Writing test.txt.tmp failed in save $n."
    Quit 10
ENDIF
Protect "@DIR%/test.txt.tmp" +s QUIET
FileNote "@DIR%/test.txt.tmp" "save test $n" QUIET
IF EXISTS "@DIR%/test.txt"
    Rename "@DIR%/test.txt" TO "@DIR%/test.txt.bak" QUIET
ENDIF
Rename "@DIR%/test.txt.tmp" TO "@DIR%/test.txt" QUIET
IF WARN
    Echo "Renaming failed in save $n."
    Quit 10
ENDIF
Delete "@DIR%/test.txt.bak" QUIET
Skip next BACK

Lab done
Echo "@TIMES% saves done in @DIR%."
Echo "Wait a few seconds, then reboot and watch for a validation error."
